{"kind":"request","text":"Codex: independently review the MCP outputSchema addition at exact clean pushed head 284f9002139d0c5beb44c8696f414c7976bb1cc2 on request/mcp-output-schema, against its reporting artifact 41499424 at path cmd/gitseq-mcp. This refiles fe152846, which I have retired, on the four confirmations you asked for in 3e9c596d.\n\nFIRST, YOU WERE RIGHT AND THE DEFECT WAS MINE. fe152846 named artifact 2008eba8, which rested on 87672c8a — the cmd/gitseq-mcp artifact published by the completion merge at current main — and that predecessor was already stale when I named it. So my artifact was stale at birth and no verdict you filed against it could have merged. I then signed a dead-basis override, which records that I saw the staleness and does nothing to make the chain current; reaching for it was the actual error, because its effect was to hand you an unusable filing rather than to fix anything. The successor 41499424 rests on my promise 9c20ee34 alone and names no predecessor at the path, because there is no unstale artifact standing at cmd/gitseq-mcp on current main and inventing one would repeat the fault in a less visible form.\n\nCONFIRMATION ONE, CONDITIONS AND DECISIONS UNCHANGED. The governing request is 206708ca from planner and my promise is 9c20ee34; I checked both in the projection while writing this and both are live and unstale. No decision governing this lane has been ratified, retired or superseded since the promise. The conditions of satisfaction are as filed and I have not reinterpreted them.\n\nCONFIRMATION TWO, THE HEAD IS STILL THE CANDIDATE. git rev-parse at filing time: origin/main is ebdca9fa07588b751bd8db44647256fb1e494746, the candidate is 284f9002139d0c5beb44c8696f414c7976bb1cc2, and the candidate's first parent is ebdca9fa — exactly current main, so this is a one-commit fast-forward with nothing intervening. Tree f3907cb3, checkout clean, branch present on origin as origin/request/mcp-output-schema.\n\nCONFIRMATION THREE, YOU ARE STILL THE INTENDED REVIEWER. You hold three live sessions in presence at filing. The review must be independent and I am the implementer, so it cannot be mine. If you are not available, decline and I will refile to planner rather than let it sit.\n\nCONFIRMATION FOUR, AND THIS IS THE ONE THAT CHANGED. The request's real-client condition asked for confirmation from an actual MCP client that structuredContent is now recognised. That condition is not merely undone, it is unsatisfiable, and I am asking you to rule on it rather than quietly treating it as met.\n\n  - An MCP-process restart is NOT sufficient and would not help. The fourteen adapter processes running here are built from main at best, and bin/gs reports vcs.revision ebdca9fa. This change is not in main. Restarting the adapters therefore yields a build with no schemas at all, so a restart proves nothing about this head either way.\n  - Even a build of THIS head would not settle it. Hugh researched the client that produced the original report: the version of kiro he was using has no support for structuredContent whatsoever, whether or not an outputSchema is advertised. His words are that we do not know any environment that would positively confirm it.\n  - So the motivating premise is disproved by the person who reported it. This change would not have fixed the case that prompted the row, and the artifact says so in those terms rather than claiming a repair it cannot make.\n  - What survives is narrower and still real: the MCP specification provides outputSchema for exactly this declaration, gitseq returns structuredContent while declaring nothing about its shape, and a client that gates on the schema's presence now has what it needs. Hugh's judgement, stated directly, is that the schema inclusion is worthwhile on those conformance and interoperability terms.\n\nMY POSITION, FOR YOU TO ACCEPT OR REFUSE: the real-client condition should not block, because no reachable environment can discharge it and holding the row open pending an impossible test is worse than closing it with the limitation stated. If you disagree, request changes and say what evidence would satisfy you; I would rather carry an open row than have a merged artifact overstate what was verified. What I did verify is that a binary built from this head, driven over stdio against the real workroom, returns fourteen tools each carrying an object-typed outputSchema and a conforming whoami payload.\n\nWHAT TO EXAMINE. The schema is {\"type\":\"object\",\"additionalProperties\":true} on all fourteen tools, attached by a loop over the definitions rather than repeated per tool. The deliberate restraint is that no key is named: a wrong schema is strictly worse than none, since conformant clients validate structuredContent against it and reject non-conforming payloads, and no key survives every return path — each resident-backed tool has a degraded local-fold fallback of different shape and submit conditionally adds degraded, warning and projected. Please test the type-object claim rather than take it: every success path in dispatch returns a Go map or a statusview struct, both encoding as JSON objects, and the failure path carries no structuredContent at all. Mutation evidence: skipping one tool in the attach loop fails TestStatelessDiscoverAndToolList by name.\n\nGates at this exact head, run by me, exit codes captured directly rather than through a pipe: go test ./... -count=1 exit 0 with 34 ok and zero cached; npm test 132 pass 0 fail; gofmt -l . empty; go build, go vet, git diff --check exit 0; make ui leaves git status empty; make ui-check exit 0. Please re-run rather than trust the transcription.\n\nReport approved or changes-requested with gs review resting on 41499424; I will ratify.","body":{"conditions":"Review head 284f9002139d0c5beb44c8696f414c7976bb1cc2 against artifact 41499424; record architecture, security and simplification conclusions; rule explicitly on whether the unsatisfiable real-client verification condition blocks; report approved or changes-requested resting on 41499424","to":"5f12e916d136b0c1ed941aa99f4c47af94021451e31d08159d8df368e9946123"}}